        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is a converging chest press?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A converging chest press is a machine whose arms move your hands closer together as you press, finishing nearer the middle of your body, rather than straight out as on a barbell or a parallel machine. The path resembles the top of a dumbbell press, but the machine loads the inward movement. Only trust the label when the maker states it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a converging chest press better than a regular chest press?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is different rather than simply better. Many lifters prefer how a converging path feels, because it resembles a dumbbell press, and makers claim it is easier on the shoulders, though that varies with your build. A parallel press is simpler and often cheaper. If you can, try both before you spend this much money.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best plate loaded chest press for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home gyms, a plate-loaded multi-press with converging arms, such as the GMWD V8, gives the most pressing angles for the money. With more room and budget, the Temple of Gainz Omnipress is more adjustable. Commercial presses from Hammer Strength, Hoist or Precor are heavier and costlier but built for daily gym use.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What does iso-lateral mean on a chest press?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Iso-lateral means each arm moves independently and carries its own load, a term Hammer Strength uses and other makers describe as independent arms. You can press with one arm, alternate arms, or press both together, and a stronger side cannot take over the load. It does not by itself mean the path converges.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I put a chest press machine upstairs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sometimes, but check first. Commercial presses can weigh 300 to over 400 pounds before plates. As general guidance, place it near a load-bearing wall, across the joists, on a platform or thick flooring, and store plates downstairs. For an older house, a long span, or any doubt, have a structural engineer look before it arrives.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a chest press machine worth it over dumbbells?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home gyms, an adjustable bench and dumbbells are better value, because they train everything and let your hands converge naturally. A converging press is worth it when you press often, train alone and want a heavy press without a spotter, or already own the basics and want the specific feel of a loaded inward path.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

